## Feature

I thought about wrapping `colpair_map()` in a function factory. This would enable users to easily produce functions with `cor_df` output and roughly `correlate()`-like syntax. Every such "manufactured" function would pass one specific function to `colpair_map()`, which would then apply it to a data frame.

```{r}
# See below for factory building notes.
as_colpair_mapper <- function(f) {
    rlang::new_function(as.pairlist(alist(data = , ... = , diagonal = NA)), 
        rlang::expr({
            colpair_map(data, .f = `!!`(f), ..., .diagonal = diagonal)
        }), rlang::caller_env())
}

# From `?colpair_map()`:
calc_p_value <- function(vec_a, vec_b) {
  t.test(vec_a, vec_b)$p.value
}

# Produce an output function:
p_value_map <- as_colpair_mapper(calc_p_value)

# This call is equivalent to `colpair_map(mtcars, calc_p_value)`:
p_value_map(mtcars)
```

The factory can also directly improve on functions like `cov()`. It basically does to such functions what `as_cordf()` does to their return value. For example, the `covary()` function below is to `cov()` roughly as `correlate()` is to `cor()`:

```{r}
covary <- as_colpair_mapper(stats::cov)

# Equivalent to `as_cordf(cov(mtcars))` and `colpair_map(mtcars, cov)`:
covary(mtcars)
```

## Factory building notes

I generated `as_colpair_mapper()` using `factory::build_factory()`, copying the output of this call (and restyling its first line):

```{r}
factory::build_factory(
  fun = function(data, ..., diagonal = NA) {
    colpair_map(data, .f = f, ..., .diagonal = diagonal)
  },
  f
)
```

See the [factory README](https://github.com/jonthegeek/factory) for the advantages of this approach over other ways of building function factories. Also note that it doesn't require factory to be a dependency of corrr.

I've started a branch in the [`tidyverse.org` repo](https://github.com/tidyverse/tidyverse.org/tree/corrr-0-4-3) for a `corrr` blog post. @thisisdaryn can you fill in the parts with new features and @jameslairdsmith can you add a section on the new function you had in #116? 

The process is to install `remotes::install_github("r-lib/hugodown")` then `hugodown::hugo_install()`. You can edit the `index.Rmd` file in `tidyverse.org/content/blog/corrr-0-4-3`  then knit to md. 

@thisisdaryn can pick the art. Make a square image from the image that you use (credit the author in the yaml) and a wide image that is 5x wider than tall (it has to be exact). Name those `thumbnail-sq.jpg` and `thumbnail-wd.jpg` then run `hugodown::use_tidy_thumbnails()` to resize them.
